/*! \page developer_template_explicit_page Implicit vs. Explicit Templates

\ref developer_template_defs_page  (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_containers_page          (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF source code contains two different types of class templates,
which we refer to as "implicit" and "explicit" templates. They differ 
in how files are organized, and in how they are treated by the compiler
and build system:

<ol type="A">
   <li>
   <b> Implicit class templates </b> : These are C++ class templates 
   for which the source code is organized so as to allow the compiler
   to perform implicit instantiation of the template as needed when 
   processing other files. This is the usual recommended way of writing 
   C++ class templates, as described in most textbooks. </li>

   <li>
   <b> Explicit class templates </b>: These are class templates that 
   are designed to be use by explicitly instantiating all required 
   specializations, and then later linking to the resulting object code. 
   </li>

</ol>
The use of an "explicit" class template is generally feasible only for 
class templates that are designed to only be used with only a small, 
foreseeable set of possible choices of template parameters. It is only in 
this case that is may be practical to explicitly instantiate all required 
specializations. 

For a standard "implicit" template, a preprocessor directive that 
includes the header file for that template must include definitions 
for all of its member functions. This can be accomplished either by 
placing all such definitions in the class header file, or by having 
the header file include a template implementation (.tpp) file that 
contains some or all of these function definitions. 

For an "explicit" class template, however, the template header file 
generally does not contain or include definitions of all member 
functions. In this case, definitions of non-inline member functions 
are instead placed in a separate file that is not included by the 
header file, much like the division of class definition and member
function definitions into separate files that is usually used for
non-templated classes.

In the PSCF code base, implicit class templates are used throughout the 
Util namespace, while many explicit templates are used in the Pscf::Prdc 
and Pscf::Rp namespaces in code that is used to model periodic systems.
The simplest use of explicit class templates in PSCF is for classes in
the Pscf::Prdc for which the the dimension of space D is the only template 
parameter. Specializations of such class template with D=1, 2 and 3 are 
compiled by explicit instantiation and later linked to create executables. 
Almost all of the class templates in namespace Pscf::Rp are explicit 
templates instead take the dimension of space as a first template 
parameter, and also take a second template parameter that is used to 
specify a choice of the computational "backend" that will be used for 
mathematical operations that are performed on large arrays of data. 
This usage is discussed in detail elsewhere.

By allowing template instantiations to be treated much like non-template
classes, the use of explicit class templates in PSCF avoids some of the
most important disadvantages of templates in C++. By reducing the amount
of template code that must be included into other files and avoiding
repeated re-compilation of each specialization, use of explicit 
instantiation tends reduce compilation times in a template-heavy code 
base. By breaking the build process into smaller steps, it also tends
to simplify debugging of errors during development, often allows the 
compiler to generate more easily interpretable error messages in 
response to syntax errors.  The main costs are the need for explicit
instantiation declarations.

\section developer_template_explicit_imp_sec A. Implicit templates

Implicit class templates are designed allow the safe use of implicit
instantiation.  All of the container class templates that are defined
in the src/util/containers directory, such as DArray and FSArray, are
standard implicit templates.

The class definition for each implicit class template is contained in a
header file that ends with file suffix .h. A preprocessor directive to
include this header file must also automatically include definitions 
of all class member functions. This requirement can be satisfied by 
organizing the code for an implicit class template in either of two ways:
<ol>

   <li> Definitions of all class member functions may be placed in the
   class template header file. </li>

   <li> Definitions of non-inline class member functions may be placed 
   in a separate template implementation file with file name suffix 
   .tpp, but this file must then be included by the header file. </li>
</ol>
Case 1 is the most common way of organizing files for a template, 
as described in most C++ textbooks and other instructional material.  

In case 2, the header file must contain a C/C++ preprocessor "#include" 
directive that includes the .tpp file, which is placed at or near the 
end of the header file. The effect is to guarantee that inclusion of 
the header file also indirectly includes the implemtentation file, thus 
creating a translation unit equivalent to that created in case 1.
In case 2, the .tpp implementation file for such an implicit template 
may never be directly included by any file other than the associated 
header file. 

\section developer_template_explicit_exp_sec B. Explicit class templates

The source code for the PSCF programs for periodic functions contains many
class templates for which there exist only a few valid choices of values
for the template parameters, and thus only a few possible valid template
specializations. Many such classes are written as whas we call "explicit" 
class templates.

The code required to use an explicit class template generally involves the
following components:

  - A class template definition, which includes declarations for all
    member functions. 

  - Definitions of inlined class template member functions

  - Definitions of non-inlined class template member functions

  - Explicit instantiations declarations for all required specializations

  - Explicit instantiation definitions for all required specializations

The class template definition is always placed in a header file with 
a base name given by the name of the class template, followed by the
file name suffix .h. This header file must also contain definitions of
any member functions that are declared "inline". 

Explicit instantiation definitions for a class template are always 
placed in a compilable source file with a file name suffix .cpp (for 
pure C++ code) or .cu (for CUDA C++ code).  This file is compiled by 
the build system to create object code for the specified template 
specializations. 

Explicit instantiation declarations must be included by any code that 
uses an instance of a template specialization in order to suppress 
implicit instantiation, which is the default behavior of C++. A 
declaration for a particular specialization instructs the compiler to 
**not** immediately compile code that specialization the first time it 
is encountered in a translation unit, and instead wait and look for 
object code for that specialization during the linking state. 

In PSCF, code for most explicit class templates is organized as follows:

  - A \*.h header file contains the class template definition, any 
    inline member functions, and explicit specialization declarations of 
    all relevant specializations that may be used elsewhere. 

  - A \*.tpp template implementation file includes the header file, 
    and contains definitions of all non-lineline member functions.

  - One or more compilable \*.cpp and/or \*.cu files include the
    implementation file (and thus indirectly include the header), and
    each contain explicit specialization definitions for several 
    specializations. 

Putting explicit specialization declarations for all relevant 
specializations in the header file guarantees that implicit instantiation
will be suppressed in any file that includes the header file.  Most of 
the class templates in the src/prdc and rp namespace have code that is 
organized as described above.
Separate \*.cpp and \*.cu files are created for classes for which it is
necessary to compile different specializations that use different 
computational backends, and that must be compiled by different compilers.

\section developer_template_explicit_example_sec Example

Class templates for which the dimension of space, D, is the only template 
parameter are always written using the simple pattern described above.  As 
an an example of this, consider the source code files associated with the 
class template Pscf::Prdc::Basis. 

The header file src/prdc/crystal/Basis.h contains a definition of the class 
template Pscf::Prdc::Basis, but does not contain definitions of any 
non-inline member functions.  The key contents of this file, in skeleton 
form, look like this:
\code
#ifndef PRDC_BASIS_H
#define PRDC_BASIS_H

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Class definition
   template <int D>
   class Basis {
      ...
   }

   // Inline member function definitions
   ...

   // External instantiation declarations
   extern template class Basis<1>;
   extern template class Basis<2>;
   extern template class Basis<3>;

}
}
#endif
\endcode
Note that the header file contains explicit instantiation declarations for 
all three possible valid specializations.

An associated template implementation file, src/rp/crystal/Basis.tpp, contains 
definitions of all non-inline member functions for the Basis class template. A 
skeleton version of this file looks something like this:
\code

#include "Basis.h"

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Non-inline member function definitions
   ....

}
}
\endcode
Note that this implementation file includes the header file. This is different
from the usage pattern for an implicit class template, which would instead 
require the header file to include the implementation file.

The compilable source file src/rp/crystal/Basis.cpp includes the file Basis.tpp 
and contains explicit instantiation definitions for D=1, D=2, and D=3:
\code
#include "Basis.tpp"

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Explicit template definitions
   template class Basis<1>;
   template class Basis<2>;
   template class Basis<3>;

}
}
\endcode
By including the implementation file (Basis.tpp), this file also indirectly 
includes the header file (Basis.h).

The PSCF build system compiles the file src/rp/crystal/Basis.cpp, places the 
resulting object code for all three valid specializations in an object file 
named Basis.o. The contents of this object file are then incorporated into a 
static library file named rplib.a. 

Separation of the code for this sort of class template into three files, as in 
the above example, is common in PSCF but is not the only possibility. For the
example shown above, definitions of the non-line fucntions could instead have 
been placed directly in the \*.cpp file, thus combining the contents of the 
*.tpp and *.cpp file. 

\section developer_template_div_special_sec Special Cases

Exceptions to the simple file organization for explict class templates that is 
described above arise primarily in two special cases:

   - Case 1: Class templates that are designed to be used only as base 
     classes for a small number of subclasses, and for which the header 
     file will only be included by the header files for these subclasses. 

   - Case 2: Class templates for which the primary template is not defined, 
     and for which all relevant specializations are instead defined as 
     explicit specializations or explicit partial specializations. 

In case 1 (class templates that are only used as base classes), explicit 
intantiations declarations for particular specializations of the base class 
in the header file of a subclass that uses those specializations, rather than
in the header file for the base class.

Case 2 arises for some class templates for which explicit partial 
specializations are defined for specializations that use CPU and GPU 
backends, as discussed elsewhere.

